There's a lot of talk about how JG Miller is just "whining" instead of
"contributing", but I think people who say this are doing themselves,
and the community, a disservice.

1)  Posting to RGRN IS contributing.  It's contributing to the
community's understanding of itself.
2)  Complaining is not a bad thing in and of itself.  Identifying any
problem, however small, is the first step to fixing it.  To use a
little hyperbole, Ghandi was nothing if not the world's biggest
whiner.
3)  If JG Miller, or anyone else, WERE planning to "do it themselves",
they'd probably post on RGRN to see if there was anyone out there who
wanted to join them

I really appreciated seeing Jim's post.  I've been wondering the same
things myself, and people like myself who have only been playing for a
couple years may not know WHY there's not been a new version in 5
years, or what anyone else in the community thinks about it.  Derek
Ray obviously thinks 5 years of almost complete silence is not healthy
for the community or the game, and JG Miller thinks so too.  So do I,
but prior to Jim or I posting here, only Derek Ray's disagreement was
public.  One of the best ways to find out who might feel the same way
is to post here.

I know that there are people who disagree with Jim, Derek, and I, but
I haven't seen too many real answers to our questions, probably
because the people defending the status quo don't have any.  They
can't, because they're not the DevTeam, and the DevTeam's not talking.

I think one of Jim's understated points is that by not talking, the
DevTeam is actively discouraging other people from taking up the flag
and marching forward.  As a programmer, if I think that there 'might'
be a new release that adds this or fixes that, I'm not going to invest
the time and effort learning the current codebase and fixing it
myself.  The problem is, as Jove pointed out today, that that new
release never arrives.

This is, I think, one of my fears, that the characteristic silence
from the DevTeam is indistinguishable from lack of progress, and after
and uncharacteristically long 5 years between releases, I don't think
it's unreasonable to consider the possibility that the latter is the
real case, whether it was the DevTeam's intentions to get to that
point or not.

So, I haven't yet looked up all of the current DevTeam's email
addresses, though some names are listed at http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Devteam,
but I'm offering my skills and time and effort to them.  If they agree
with anything I've said, or think that I might understand why they
disagree, and be capable of moving forward with work from that point,
I'm available.  There might be novel ways of handling two-way
communication between the DevTeam and the Community. While "official"
communication from the DevTeam could still be effectively nil, more
frequent, if not regular, unofficial "The mail & loses his pouch!"
letters could be posted.  Who knows, even something as quirky as wand
engraving could be effective.  Maybe I could swing them a
basecamphq.com account, or get NetHack highlighted at a gaming expo.
Just thinking about it reminds me of Jim's earlier point:  There's a
pool of talent in the community that is untapped, only because in the
end, we DO have confidence the DevTeam and would like to support them
and the game, but there's currently no known channel by which to do
it.

